cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "CoRTeC: A Differentially Private Release for Synthetic Tabular Data, Decodable by Any Generator"
message: "If you use this software or the accompanying paper, please cite it."
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: "1.2.5"
date-released: "2026-10-30"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22945315"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Calyie/cortec"
abstract: >-
  Hospitals and other institutions want to share synthetic versions of their tables without
  exposing the people in them. We propose CoRTeC, a method that does this under differential
  privacy. CoRTeC spends the privacy budget once, on a small file of noisy counts designed for
  training a classifier. For each group of records the file gives the group's size, its balance of
  outcomes, a histogram of every column for each outcome and the outcome rate in a set of
  non-overlapping cells. A reviewer can check every number before the file leaves the institution.
  Anyone can then turn the counts into synthetic rows, as often as they like and at no further
  privacy cost. A maximum-entropy decoder does this without any language model. So can each of the
  five frozen language models we tested, and none of them ever sees a real record. A selection
  step then matches the rows to the released counts. Decoded without a language model, the
  automatically configured release at ε = 2 trains classifiers to within 0.02 AUC of a real sample
  of the same size on the Adult dataset over three splits. The gap is larger on NHANES and bank
  marketing, reaching 0.07 AUC on the medical data. Because the counts are fixed in advance, the
  release reproduces the outcome rates a deployment asks for, at some cost to pairs of columns.
  Four controls show that most of the utility comes from the release and the selection step, and
  that a language model adds what it knows from the column names. No private row ever reaches a
  model, and no synthetic row copies a real one. The strongest membership attack stays far below
  the bound the budget allows. This repository holds the Apache-2.0 reference implementation with
  its tests, the research pipeline, the paper, the deployment guide and the frozen
  auto-configurations.
authors:
